  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Saturday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A ridge of high pressure will pass\neastward over the Silver State this afternoon and tonight. An\nEastern Pacific upper-level area of low pressure will make\nlandfall in Central California Monday. Southwesterly flow ahead of\nthis low pressure system may pump enough atmospheric moisture\nnorthward into Central Nevada Monday to support the formation of\nisolated thunderstorms. A few of these thunderstorms could be\nstrong. The primary thunderstorm hazards will be wind gusts near\n50 mph and dangerous cloud to ground lightning strikes. \n\nThe aforementioned cyclone aloft will open up into a trough of low\npressure as it moves northeastward across Eastern Nevada Tuesday,\nresulting in the likelihood of light rainfall. White Pine County\nmay receive wetting rainfall, but rain amounts is other locations\nare expected to remain below one tenth of an inch. A ridge of \nhigh pressure will build northeastward across the Silver State \nWednesday and Thursday, resulting in a warming trend. By Thursday \nafternoon, high temperatures throughout Northern and Central \nNevada will be six to eight degrees above normal for this time of \nyear. Breezes should increase Friday and Saturday as the surface \npressure gradient tightens. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High forecast confidence in the\npossibility of a few strong thunderstorms in Central Nevada \nMonday afternoon into Monday evening. Moderate forecast confidence\nregarding the likelihood of light rainfall in Eastern Nevada \nTuesday. Moderate forecast confidence in a warming trend Wednesday\nand Thursday. No deviations from the NBM model output.",
